  "id": "646547815",
  "subject": [
    "ANE",
    "Babylonian"
  ],
  "title": "Ekitu\u0161garza",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/unlocated",
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/temple-2"
  ],
  "details": "<p>To differentiate the two B\u0113let-Eanna temples in Babylon, inscriptions of the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 604\u2013652 BC) specify that Ekitu\u0161garza, which was in the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/89975947\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Tuba</a> district, was built \u201cin the recess of the city wall of Babylon\u201d (see, for example, RIBo Babylon 7 <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/ribo/babylon7/Q005473\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Nebuchadnezzar II 002</a> iv 44\u201348); her other temple Ekitusgirzal, which was in the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/54136921\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">New City </a>district, was built \u201con the canal bank.\u201d As noted already by A.R. George (1992: 330), \u201cThe \"recess of the city wall\" rather suggests a place where the wall changed direction (<i>tubqu</i> also has the meaning \"corner\", at the point where two walls meet), but there were no such places in the quarter Tuba, where <i>Tintir</i> sites E-kitu\u0161-garza. The reference may therefore be merely to a corner made by a projecting tower, such as would undoubtedly have formed part of the monumental <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/386554382\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">\u0160ama\u0161 Gate</a>. For this reason the temple is shown on our sketch map (fig. 4 on p. 24) as adjacent to this city gate, but the location is, of course, very tentative.\u201d</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The temple of the goddess B\u0113let-Eanna, Ekitu\u0161garza (\u201cHouse, Abode of the Regulations\u201d), was located in the Tuba district of Babylon, in the western half of the inner city. According to the Babylonian topographical text Tintir = Babylon Tablet IV, it was one of three temples in that part of Babylon. Ekitu\u0161garza was rebuilt by the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 604\u2013652 BC).",
